.\" lsof-rs(1): the memory-safe lsof. Kept short: the options are lsof(8)'s
.\" (Lsof.8 in the C tree) and `lsof -h`; this page says what lsof-rs does
.\" differently, each item measured against the C lsof 4.99.6 on glibc 2.39.
.TH LSOF-RS 1 2026-11-19 "lsof-rs 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsof-rs \- list open files, a memory-safe lsof
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsof
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIpath\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsof
from lsof-rs lists the files processes have open, as the C
.BR lsof (8)
does, with the C's options, output formats (the table,
.B \-F
fields,
.B \-J
and
.B \-j
JSON) and exit status.
It runs on Linux, Windows and FreeBSD.
.B lsof \-h
lists the options.
As in the C, the options end at the first word that opens with neither
.B \-
nor
.BR + ,
or is
.B \-
or
.B +
alone, which is the first
.IR path ,
and at
.B \-\-
or
.BR ++ ;
every word after is a
.IR path ,
so
.B "lsof f \-p 1"
names three files.
.PP
Its output is meant to be the C's byte for byte.
On Linux a differential harness runs both on the same processes and
compares them; every difference it finds is fixed or recorded in
.IR DIVERGENCES.md ,
with the reason.
.SS Repeat mode
.B \-r
.RI [ t [\fBc\fIn\fR][\fBm\fIfmt\fR]]
and
.B +r
repeat as the C's do:
.I t
and
.I n
are read as the C's
.I int
reads them, wrapping
.RB ( "\-r 4294967297"
is a delay of 1 second);
.I fmt
is glibc's
.BR strftime (3)
in the C locale, its
.B %s
glibc's
.BR mktime (3);
with
.B TZ
unset,
.I /etc/localtime
is looked at again at each marker and read again where it changed;
a zone that names daylight time and no rule reads
.IR posixrules .
Standard output is buffered as glibc buffers it and flushed where the C
flushes it.
A closed pipe kills
.B lsof
with
.B SIGPIPE
at the write that meets it, unless the signal was ignored or blocked when
it started; then, as on a full disk, the write is dropped, nothing is said,
and the run goes on and exits with its usual status.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM THE C LSOF
These are deliberate, and each is user-visible.
.TP
.B Escaped arguments in messages
Every message that quotes an argument escapes its control bytes and the
bytes that are not UTF-8, as the C's
.B safestrprt()
does (\fB^[\fR for ESC, \fB\\x\fINN\fR otherwise).
The C prints some arguments raw: the option letter in
.BR "illegal option character" ,
.BR \-i 's
protocol, host and service names,
.BR \-K ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-e ,
.B \-c
conflicts,
.B "avoiding stat(P): \-b was specified"
and
.BR +m 's
lines.
A name holding an escape sequence cannot drive the terminal of whoever
reads the message.
.TP
.B A zone file that is no regular file
When the zone file
.B TZ
or
.I /etc/localtime
names is a FIFO, a device or anything else but a regular file, glibc opens
it and reads it without a time limit: a FIFO with no writer holds the C for
ever.
.B lsof
opens it without blocking and waits at most 3 seconds in all for what glibc
could use of it (a version 1 zone file's data); after that it is taken as
unreadable, as glibc takes a file that ends short.
Where the data comes in time the zone is the C's.
On FreeBSD such a file is not read at all.
A regular zone file that another process holds a write lease on is
waited for the same 3 seconds (its open tried again every 10 ms), where
glibc's open waits until the holder lets go or the kernel breaks the lease
.RI ( /proc/sys/fs/lease-break-time ,
45 seconds by default).
.TP
.B Accounts where glibc would crash
A login is the C library's, as for the C, but where
.I /etc/nsswitch.conf
has a shape in which glibc 2.39 could misbehave, on any line for the
database, not only the last: a
.B passwd:
line with no source (glibc reads past a heap block); under
.BR compat ,
a
.B netgroup:
or
.B passwd_compat:
line with no source (the same read); or
.B compat
in both
.B passwd:
and
.B passwd_compat:
(glibc recurses until its stack is gone, and the C dies).
There, and for an entry that would not fit in 16 MiB,
.B lsof
reads
.I /etc/passwd
itself.
.TP
.B A /proc that is another PID namespace's
Run in a PID namespace whose
.I /proc
is still another's
.RB ( "unshare \-p \-f"
without
.BR \-\-mount\-proc ,
a container given the host's
.IR /proc ),
the C cannot find itself there: it prints no offset, no file flags and no
eventfd, pidfd or eventpoll identity, drops
.B \-o
with a warning, and dies on the first eventpoll it reads.
.B lsof
reads each descriptor's fdinfo there as anywhere, and prints what both
print on the host.
.TP
.B Version
.B \-v
prints one line, the lsof-rs version, where the C prints a report of its
build.
.TP
.B SELinux contexts
.B \-Z
is refused where SELinux is enabled
.RB ( "\-Z (SELinux context) is not implemented" ),
where the C prints a CONTEXT column.
.TP
.B FreeBSD
The options are those of FreeBSD's C lsof
.RB ( \-K
lists threads with a TID column;
.B +M
and
.B \-M
are read;
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-E ,
.B \-X
and
.B \-Z
are no options there), with these differences.
No device cache file is read or written: the C keeps
.IR /dev 's
table in a file in the home directory and says so when it creates or
updates it, and
.B lsof
reads
.I /dev
on every run.
.B \-D
is read as the C reads it and changes nothing;
.B "\-D ?"
reports that there is no cache path.
.B \-k
and
.B \-m
are
.BR "unsupported option" :
nothing is read from kernel memory.
A socket's host and port are always numbers, as under
.BR "\-n \-P" .
On a run that names a path,
.B lsof
makes its bounded calls in a helper process, as the C does in a child;
the helper has
.I /dev/null
as descriptor 2 where the C's child has none, so a listing that holds it
shows that one row more.
.TP
.B Windows
A closed pipe ends the run with exit status 141, as there is no
.B SIGPIPE
to inherit; other write errors are dropped, as on Unix.
.BR \-\-etw ,
.B \-\-unicode
and
.B \-\-ascii
are options there (the ETW capture; the console switched to UTF-8 once
the options are read, the last of the two deciding), read only where an
option word stands: after a
.IR path ,
.B \-\-
or
.BR ++ ,
or given back by an option
.RB ( "\-L \-\-etw" ),
each is read as the C reads it.
Elsewhere they are no options, as in the C:
.B "lsof \-\-etw f"
is
.B "illegal option character: \-"
and
.BR \-e 's
error for
.BR tw ,
exit 1.
.PP
.I DIVERGENCES.md
in the source tree records every known difference, these and those that
arise only from the C's undefined behaviour or its defects.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ, TZDIR
The zone of
.BR \-r 's
marker, read as glibc's
.BR tzset (3)
reads them.
.TP
.B RES_OPTIONS
Read as glibc's resolver reads it; host names are looked up as the C looks
them up unless
.B \-n
is given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every search item was found, 1 otherwise or on an error, as the C's;
.B "\-c ^"
with no name excludes every command, so nothing is listed and the status
is 1, as the C's;
.B +r
exits 1 when its first cycle lists nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsof (8),
.BR strftime (3),
.BR tzset (3)
